                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Before touching the VN, I read the manga and was quite disturbed by the amount of fan service in it, especially since every single character from the main cast is a minor, below 15 if I remember correctly... \nAnd that's exactly why no words can express how GLAD I am that the Visual Novel has crude jokes that the characters say to each other AT MOST! No weird panty shots or showing off chests of teenage girls right in your face! \nIt's sad that the bar is low enough to get happy at something as seemingly simple as that, but you can never know what you get with japanese VNs and media in general.\n\nI've been a fan of the franchise for.. maybe three years, and after playing the VN itself, I can confidently say that I will stay a fan for even longer. I mean, it's just THAT good!\nThe game starts off slow and takes a while to build up, sure, but because of exactly that, the horror is so incredibly satisfying once you get to it! The hours that you spend doing club activities with no horror involved however isn't filler, it serves as a way to help you get to know the characters, which I think is amazing. It makes you grow attached to the main cast, for me personally, I grew really attached to Mion.\nI truly love how the protagonist, Keiichi, treats his friends like actual people rather than objectifying them like a lot of other japanese VN male protagonists, especially considering how his friends are all women.\nDid I mention that I also adore the way the characters are written? They're all truly likeable and have their own complexities and stories rather than just being an asset to push the story forward. It's really refreshing to see.\n\nI genuinely recommend this game to anyone who likes horror that starts off cutesy, has watched the anime or read the manga, or likes some good mystery.",
